---
import CaseStudyLayout from '../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  title="Architecture Reference — Da Vinci Studio Cinematic System"
  description="Complete engineering reference for the Da Vinci Studio cinematic portfolio system. Engine contracts, ownership model, scroll architecture, pointer engine, observability layer, and performance budget."
  ogImage="/og/architecture.svg"
  canonical="https://davincistudio.ae/architecture"
>

  <!-- Navigation -->
  <nav class="cs-nav">
    <a href="/case-study" class="cs-nav-back">← Back to case study</a>
    <span class="cs-nav-title">Architecture Reference</span>
    <span class="cs-nav-tag">rev. 13 · 2026</span>
  </nav>

  <main id="main-content" class="cs-page">

    <!-- ── Header ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <header class="cs-hero">
      <div class="cs-hero-eyeline">
        <span class="cs-eyeline-dash"></span>
        <span class="cs-eyeline-text">Da Vinci Studio · Engineering Reference</span>
      </div>

      <h1 class="cs-hero-title">
        System<br />
        <span>Architecture</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="cs-hero-sub">
        Complete engineering reference for the cinematic portfolio system.
        Engine contracts, ownership rules, scroll architecture, interaction
        pipeline, observability design, and production constraints. Written
        for engineers and studios who want the full picture.
      </p>

      <div class="cs-hero-meta">
        <div class="cs-meta-item">
          <span class="cs-meta-label">Revision</span>
          <span class="cs-meta-value">13 (Phase 13 — productisation)</span>
        </div>
        <div class="cs-meta-item">
          <span class="cs-meta-label">Stack</span>
          <span class="cs-meta-value">Astro 6 · React 19 · Vite 6</span>
        </div>
        <div class="cs-meta-item">
          <span class="cs-meta-label">Runtime animation</span>
          <span class="cs-meta-value">CSS transitions + requestAnimationFrame</span>
        </div>
        <div class="cs-meta-item">
          <span class="cs-meta-label">External animation</span>
          <span class="cs-meta-value">None (no GSAP, no Framer Motion)</span>
        </div>
        <div class="cs-meta-item">
          <span class="cs-meta-label">Scroll</span>
          <span class="cs-meta-value">Lenis v1 (inertial smoothing only)</span>
        </div>
        <div class="cs-meta-item">
          <span class="cs-meta-label">3D</span>
          <span class="cs-meta-value">Three.js r169 (bundled, low-power)</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- ── §1 · Core Design Principle ────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="cs-section">
      <div class="cs-section-eyeline">
        <span class="cs-section-num">§1</span>
        <span class="cs-section-label">Core Design Principle</span>
      </div>

      <h2 class="cs-h2">Single ownership</h2>

      <p class="cs-p">
        Every running loop, every event listener, and every RAF tick in the
        system has exactly one owner. No component adds its own scroll
        listener. No component starts its own RAF loop independently.
        The ownership model is the architecture.
      </p>

      <p class="cs-p">
        This rule was not imposed at the start — it emerged as the system
        grew and the cost of ambiguous ownership became visible. After Phase 9
        (lifecycle hardening), it was formalised as a constraint: any future
        change that requires introducing a new scroll listener at the component
        level is a design error, not an implementation choice.
      </p>

      <div class="cs-callout">
        <p>
          <strong>Ownership rule:</strong> If two components could reasonably
          both own a given resource, that resource belongs to neither — it
          belongs to the engine layer. Components subscribe; they do not create.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── §2 · Directory Structure ──────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="cs-section">
      <div class="cs-section-eyeline">
        <span class="cs-section-num">§2</span>
        <span class="cs-section-label">Directory Structure</span>
      </div>

      <h2 class="cs-h2">Source map</h2>

      <div class="cs-diagram">
        <div class="cs-diagram-label">src/ tree — annotated</div>
        <pre>
src/
├── <span class="hl">boot/</span>                         ← startup sequencing (no React, no DOM)
│   ├── BootSystem.ts             ← cinematic loader, status sequence, 2.38s boot
│   ├── CinematicOrchestrator.ts  ← single startup authority; enforces init order
│   └── CinematicApp.tsx          ← legacy; scene registration now in orchestrator
│
├── <span class="hl">core/</span>                         ← runtime engines (pure TypeScript singletons)
│   ├── scroll/
│   │   └── ScrollEngine.ts       ← Lenis wrapper; RAF loop; visibilitychange guard
│   ├── interaction/
│   │   ├── PointerEngine.ts      ← global mousemove → LERP → subscribers
│   │   └── usePointer.ts         ← React hook: subscribe + cleanup
│   ├── scene-engine/
│   │   ├── SceneController.ts    ← scroll→state machine; 6 scene registrations
│   │   ├── SceneRegistry.ts      ← scroll range definitions (start/end/soft boundary)
│   │   ├── SceneWrapper.tsx      ← HOC; mounts scene, passes state down
│   │   ├── useScene.ts           ← React hook: SceneState subscription
│   │   └── useSceneProgress.ts   ← React hook: normalised 0–1 progress
│   └── observability/
│       ├── CinematicMonitor.ts   ← DEV-only: FPS, spike log, health flags
│       └── DebugOverlay.tsx      ← DEV-only: Ctrl+Shift+D HUD
│
├── <span class="hl">scenes/</span>                       ← content layers (React, scroll-driven)
│   ├── Hero/
│   │   ├── HeroScene.tsx         ← entry hero; pointer depth; cinematic-entry gate
│   │   └── HeroScene.css
│   ├── Philosophy/
│   │   └── PhilosophyScene.tsx
│   ├── Services/
│   │   └── ServicesScene.tsx     ← bento grid; per-card reveal stagger
│   ├── Work/
│   │   └── WorkScene.tsx         ← project list; hover row dimming
│   ├── Logos/
│   │   └── LogosScene.tsx        ← trust tools; stagger entry on scroll
│   └── CTA/
│       └── CTAScene.tsx          ← pointer button lift; availability badge
│
├── <span class="hl">visual/</span>                       ← atmosphere layers (non-semantic, aria-hidden)
│   ├── three/
│   │   └── ThreeCanvas.tsx       ← WebGL mandala sphere; low-power; reduced-motion skip
│   ├── atmosphere/
│   │   ├── DotGrid.tsx           ← Canvas dot field; static draw; CSS drift
│   │   ├── DepthLayer.tsx        ← scroll-driven vignette + perspective blind
│   │   └── CursorField.tsx       ← radial glow at cursor; coarse-pointer no-op
│   └── signature/
│       └── HeroSignature.tsx     ← scanline, focus zone, corner brackets
│
├── <span class="hl">styles/</span>
│   ├── global.css                ← resets, :root tokens, scrollbar
│   ├── scenes.css                ← all scene CSS (1948 lines); cinematic easing token
│   └── case-study.css            ← document CSS for /case-study and /architecture
│
├── <span class="hl">layouts/</span>
│   ├── Layout.astro              ← main app shell; mounts React island
│   └── CaseStudyLayout.astro     ← document shell for prose pages
│
├── <span class="hl">pages/</span>
│   ├── index.astro               ← production entry (cinematic experience)
│   ├── case-study.astro          ← design+engineering case study
│   └── architecture.astro        ← this document
│
└── <span class="hl">data/</span>
    └── projects.ts               ← Project[] type + array
        </pre>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── §3 · Startup Pipeline ──────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="cs-section">
      <div class="cs-section-eyeline">
        <span class="cs-section-num">§3</span>
        <span class="cs-section-label">Startup Pipeline</span>
      </div>

      <h2 class="cs-h2">CinematicOrchestrator</h2>

      <p class="cs-p">
        Before Phase 6 (CinematicOrchestrator introduction), each engine
        initialised itself when its component mounted. The ordering was
        implicit and depended on React's render order — which is not
        guaranteed. The result was a boot sequence that worked most of the
        time and failed silently the rest.
      </p>

      <p class="cs-p">
        CinematicOrchestrator replaced this with a single authority class
        that enforces strict ordering. App.tsx now has zero engine knowledge —
        its useEffect is a single <code style="font-family:var(--f-mono);font-size:0.85em;color:var(--c-cyan)">CinematicOrchestrator.run(callbacks)</code>
        call.
      </p>

      <div class="cs-diagram">
        <div class="cs-diagram-label">Startup sequence — deterministic ordering</div>
        <pre>
<span class="hl">CinematicOrchestrator.run(callbacks)</span>    ← called once from App.tsx useEffect
    │
    ├─ 1. register scenes ────────────────→ SceneController.register() × 6
    │                                        (hero, philosophy, services,
    │                                         work, logos, cta)
    │
    ├─ 2. initialize scroll ──────────────→ <span class="hl">ScrollEngine.initialize()</span>
    │      try/catch — failure non-fatal       Lenis created, RAF attached
    │      9s fallback timer still runs        Lenis STOPPED (not started yet)
    │
    ├─ 3. mount pointer ──────────────────→ <span class="hl">PointerEngine.mount()</span>
    │      try/catch — failure non-fatal       mousemove (passive), RAF started
    │      coarse-pointer check on mount       skips entirely if touch device
    │
    ├─ 4. callbacks.setDomReady(true) ────→ React re-render: app DOM mounts
    │                                        (hidden behind loader overlay)
    │
    ├─ 5. BootSystem.boot() ─────────────→ <span class="hl-v">status sequence (2.38s)</span>
    │         DA VINCI STUDIO      1.1s
    │         INITIALISING         1.58s
    │         LOADING ASSETS       2.06s
    │         ENTERING             2.70s  ← fires _triggerEntry()
    │
    └─ 6. _triggerEntry()
              │
              ├─ ScrollEngine.start()    ← Lenis begins running
              ├─ callbacks.setAppReady() ← loader fade-out begins
              └─ dispatchEvent('cinematic-entry')
                      │
                      ├─ HeroScene: 200ms delay → setEntered(true) → text reveal
                      └─ App.tsx: atmosphereReady gate opens → ThreeCanvas mounts
        </pre>
      </div>

      <div class="cs-callout">
        <p>
          <strong>Fallback timer:</strong> A 9-second
          <code style="font-family:var(--f-mono);font-size:0.85em">setTimeout</code>
          in CinematicOrchestrator calls <code style="font-family:var(--f-mono);font-size:0.85em">_triggerEntry()</code>
          unconditionally. If BootSystem fails for any reason, the app always
          becomes visible at 9 seconds. The fallback emits a
          <code style="font-family:var(--f-mono);font-size:0.85em">console.warn</code>
          — the only non-dev log retained in production, because it signals
          a genuine timing anomaly.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── §4 · Scene Engine ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="cs-section">
      <div class="cs-section-eyeline">
        <span class="cs-section-num">§4</span>
        <span class="cs-section-label">Scene Engine</span>
      </div>

      <h2 class="cs-h2">SceneController</h2>

      <p class="cs-p">
        SceneController is a TypeScript singleton that maps normalised scroll
        progress (0–1) to scene states for all six registered scenes. It has
        no React dependency — it is a pure state machine that React components
        subscribe to.
      </p>

      <h3 class="cs-h3">Scene states</h3>

      <div class="cs-principles">
        <div class="cs-principle">
          <span class="cs-principle-num">STATE</span>
          <div class="cs-principle-title">inactive</div>
          <p class="cs-principle-body">
            Scroll progress is more than 5% outside the scene's range.
            Scene is <code style="font-family:var(--f-mono);font-size:0.8em;color:var(--c-cyan)">opacity:0</code>,
            <code style="font-family:var(--f-mono);font-size:0.8em;color:var(--c-cyan)">blur:8–10px</code>,
            translated off-screen. Not composited.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="cs-principle">
          <span class="cs-principle-num">STATE</span>
          <div class="cs-principle-title">secondary</div>
          <p class="cs-principle-body">
            Scroll progress is within the soft 5% boundary around the scene's
            range. Scene is partially visible (opacity 0.25–0.30), slightly
            blurred (2–4px), partially translated. Creates cross-dissolve effect.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="cs-principle">
          <span class="cs-principle-num">STATE</span>
          <div class="cs-principle-title">primary</div>
          <p class="cs-principle-body">
            Scroll progress is within the scene's exact range [start, end].
            Scene is fully visible (opacity 1), unblurred (0px),
            at rest position. Full pointer interaction enabled.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3 class="cs-h3" style="margin-top:40px">Scene scroll ranges</h3>

      <div class="cs-diagram">
        <div class="cs-diagram-label">SCENE_REGISTRY — scroll ranges (normalised 0–1)</div>
        <pre>
Scene         start    end     range     notes
────────────  ───────  ──────  ────────  ──────────────────────────────────────
<span class="hl">hero</span>          0.00     0.15    0.15      first scene; gated by cinematic-entry
<span class="hl-v">philosophy</span>    0.12     0.35    0.23      overlaps hero — soft transition begins early
<span class="hl">services</span>      0.32     0.55    0.23      bento grid; per-card stagger on first primary
<span class="hl-v">work</span>          0.52     0.78    0.26      longest range; table hover interaction
<span class="hl">logos</span>         0.75     0.90    0.15      trust layer; stagger on entry
<span class="hl-v">cta</span>           0.88     1.00    0.12      final scene; pointer button lift
        </pre>
      </div>

      <div class="cs-callout violet">
        <p>
          <strong>Overlap is intentional:</strong> Philosophy starts at 0.12
          while Hero ends at 0.15. During 0.10–0.17 both scenes are in
          <code style="font-family:var(--f-mono);font-size:0.85em">secondary</code>
          state simultaneously. This produces a cross-dissolve rather than a
          cut — no frame is empty, no scene appears before its predecessor
          has begun fading.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3 class="cs-h3" style="margin-top:40px">React integration</h3>

      <p class="cs-p">
        SceneController notifies subscribers only on state changes
        (<code style="font-family:var(--f-mono);font-size:0.85em;color:var(--c-cyan)">inactive → secondary</code>,
        <code style="font-family:var(--f-mono);font-size:0.85em;color:var(--c-cyan)">secondary → primary</code>,
        etc.) — not on every scroll frame. React re-renders happen at scene
        boundaries only. The pointer effects and translateY values derived
        from scroll progress are applied via direct DOM style mutation on refs,
        not via setState.
      </p>

      <div class="cs-diagram">
        <div class="cs-diagram-label">State notification model</div>
        <pre>
ScrollEngine RAF (~60fps)
    └→ SceneController.update(progress)     ← called every frame
           │
           for each scene: calculateState(scene, progress)
           │
           if (newState !== scene.prevState):   ← state changed
               scene.onStateChange(newState)    ← SceneWrapper setState()  → React re-render
               scene.prevState = newState
           │
           always:
               SceneController.frameListeners   ← notified every frame
               (DepthLayer uses this for parallax)
        </pre>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── §5 · Scroll Engine ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="cs-section">
      <div class="cs-section-eyeline">
        <span class="cs-section-num">§5</span>
        <span class="cs-section-label">Scroll Engine</span>
      </div>

      <h2 class="cs-h2">ScrollEngine</h2>

      <p class="cs-p">
        ScrollEngine wraps Lenis v1 with lifecycle management, resilience
        additions, and a clean contract for the rest of the system.
        Lenis is not imported anywhere else — all scroll interaction goes
        through ScrollEngine.
      </p>

      <h3 class="cs-h3">Configuration</h3>

      <div class="cs-diagram">
        <div class="cs-diagram-label">Lenis configuration</div>
        <pre>
new Lenis(&#123;
  duration:  1.4,           ← inertial duration; feels substantial without fighting user
  easing:    t =&gt; 1 - Math.pow(1 - t, 3),   ← cubic ease-out; matches visual weight
  infinite:  false,         ← finite scroll — no wrapping
  direction: 'vertical',
&#125;)
        </pre>
      </div>

      <h3 class="cs-h3" style="margin-top:40px">Resilience additions</h3>

      <div class="cs-principles">
        <div class="cs-principle">
          <span class="cs-principle-num">GUARD</span>
          <div class="cs-principle-title">visibilitychange handler</div>
          <p class="cs-principle-body">
            When the tab is hidden, <code style="font-family:var(--f-mono);font-size:0.8em;color:var(--c-cyan)">lenis.stop()</code>
            is called. When it returns, <code style="font-family:var(--f-mono);font-size:0.8em;color:var(--c-cyan)">lenis.start()</code>
            resumes. Without this, the browser's RAF suspension during hide
            creates a large time gap — Lenis advances scroll position in
            a single jump on resume.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="cs-principle">
          <span class="cs-principle-num">GUARD</span>
          <div class="cs-principle-title">document.hidden check on start()</div>
          <p class="cs-principle-body">
            <code style="font-family:var(--f-mono);font-size:0.8em;color:var(--c-cyan)">ScrollEngine.start()</code>
            checks <code style="font-family:var(--f-mono);font-size:0.8em;color:var(--c-cyan)">document.hidden</code>
            before calling <code style="font-family:var(--f-mono);font-size:0.8em;color:var(--c-cyan)">lenis.start()</code>.
            If the tab is hidden at boot time (background tab open),
            Lenis is not started — the visibilitychange handler starts
            it when the tab becomes visible.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="cs-principle">
          <span class="cs-principle-num">GUARD</span>
          <div class="cs-principle-title">try/catch initialisation</div>
          <p class="cs-principle-body">
            ScrollEngine.initialize() is wrapped in a try/catch inside
            CinematicOrchestrator. A Lenis failure is non-fatal — the
            9-second fallback timer fires, the app becomes visible with
            native scroll only.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── §6 · Pointer Engine ────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="cs-section">
      <div class="cs-section-eyeline">
        <span class="cs-section-num">§6</span>
        <span class="cs-section-label">Pointer Engine</span>
      </div>

      <h2 class="cs-h2">PointerEngine</h2>

      <p class="cs-p">
        A single global <code style="font-family:var(--f-mono);font-size:0.85em;color:var(--c-cyan)">mousemove</code>
        listener feeds raw coordinates into a delta-time-normalised LERP
        loop. Subscribers receive a smoothed pointer snapshot per frame.
        Zero per-element listeners. Zero React re-renders from pointer movement.
      </p>

      <h3 class="cs-h3">LERP specification</h3>

      <div class="cs-diagram">
        <div class="cs-diagram-label">Delta-time normalised LERP</div>
        <pre>
<span class="dim">// LERP_60 = 0.055 — produces 90% decay in ~40 frames at 60fps = ~667ms</span>
<span class="dim">// dt = time since last RAF tick, clamped to 100ms (prevents tab-resume jumps)</span>

<span class="hl">factor = 1 - Math.pow(1 - LERP_60, dt / 16.667)</span>

current.x += (raw.x - current.x) * factor
current.y += (raw.y - current.y) * factor

<span class="dim">// At 30fps: factor ≈ 0.10 — same wall-clock convergence rate as 60fps</span>
<span class="dim">// At 120fps: factor ≈ 0.028 — same wall-clock convergence rate as 60fps</span>
<span class="dim">// Without dt-normalisation: at 120fps motion would be twice as fast</span>
        </pre>
      </div>

      <h3 class="cs-h3" style="margin-top:40px">Output values</h3>

      <div class="cs-diagram">
        <div class="cs-diagram-label">PointerState shape</div>
        <pre>
interface PointerState &#123;
  <span class="hl">x</span>:  number;   ← smoothed absolute clientX (pixels)
  <span class="hl">y</span>:  number;   ← smoothed absolute clientY (pixels)
  <span class="hl">cx</span>: number;   ← normalised -1→+1 (0 = viewport center X)
  <span class="hl">cy</span>: number;   ← normalised -1→+1 (0 = viewport center Y)
&#125;
        </pre>
      </div>

      <h3 class="cs-h3" style="margin-top:40px">Scene intensity</h3>

      <p class="cs-p">
        Scenes read <code style="font-family:var(--f-mono);font-size:0.85em;color:var(--c-cyan)">PointerEngine.sceneIntensity(sceneId)</code>
        to scale their pointer effects. This prevents inactive scenes from
        reacting to the pointer — a scene in the background should not
        move because the user is hovering over a visible scene.
      </p>

      <div class="cs-diagram">
        <div class="cs-diagram-label">sceneIntensity() return values</div>
        <pre>
state = 'primary'    → <span class="hl">1.0</span>   ← full effect
state = 'secondary'  → <span class="hl-v">0.3</span>   ← reduced (scene is transitioning out)
state = 'inactive'   → <span class="dim">0.0</span>   ← no effect (early return, no transform applied)
        </pre>
      </div>

      <h3 class="cs-h3" style="margin-top:40px">Guards</h3>

      <div class="cs-before-after">
        <div class="cs-ba-col before">
          <div class="cs-ba-label before">Coarse pointer guard</div>
          <div class="cs-ba-value before" style="font-size:0.9rem;margin-bottom:8px;">
            (pointer: coarse)
          </div>
          <div class="cs-ba-desc">
            PointerEngine checks <code style="font-family:var(--f-mono);font-size:0.8em">matchMedia('(pointer: coarse)')</code>
            on mount. If true, returns before registering any listeners or
            starting the RAF loop. Touch devices receive zero pointer-system
            overhead — not degraded, absent.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="cs-ba-arrow">+</div>
        <div class="cs-ba-col after">
          <div class="cs-ba-label after">Reduced-motion guard</div>
          <div class="cs-ba-value after" style="font-size:0.9rem;margin-bottom:8px;">
            prefers-reduced-motion
          </div>
          <div class="cs-ba-desc">
            PointerEngine reads
            <code style="font-family:var(--f-mono);font-size:0.8em">matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)')</code>
            and tracks live changes. When active, subscriber callbacks are
            skipped on each RAF tick — position tracking continues (getState()
            stays accurate) but no DOM transforms are applied.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── §7 · Visual Layer Stack ────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="cs-section">
      <div class="cs-section-eyeline">
        <span class="cs-section-num">§7</span>
        <span class="cs-section-label">Visual Layer Stack</span>
      </div>

      <h2 class="cs-h2">Atmosphere architecture</h2>

      <p class="cs-p">
        The visual system is composed of seven independent layers, each with
        its own z-index plane, rendering technology, and update mechanism.
        No layer depends on another at runtime — they are composited by the
        browser.
      </p>

      <div class="cs-diagram">
        <div class="cs-diagram-label">Complete layer stack — z-index ascending</div>
        <pre>
z     Layer             Component           Technology          Update mechanism
────  ────────────────  ──────────────────  ──────────────────  ──────────────────────────────
<span class="dim"> 0</span>    Dot grid          DotGrid.tsx         Canvas 2D           Static draw on mount;
<span class="dim">   </span>                                                              CSS translate drift only
<span class="hl"> 1</span>    WebGL sphere      ThreeCanvas.tsx     WebGL / Three.js    RAF rotation loop;
<span class="hl">   </span>                                                              opacity 0.30; low-power ctx
<span class="dim"> 3</span>    Depth vignette    DepthLayer.tsx      CSS gradients       ScrollEngine frameListener;
<span class="dim">   </span>                                                              opacity 0→0.85 with scroll
<span class="dim"> 3</span>    Cursor field      CursorField.tsx     CSS radial-gradient PointerEngine subscriber;
<span class="dim">   </span>                                                              translate follows cursor
