      <h2 class="cs-h2">CinematicMonitor</h2>

      <p class="cs-p">
        All production bugs were found by reading code and reasoning about
        behavior, not by instrumenting production traffic. The observability
        layer was built for development confidence, not production monitoring.
        Its production cost is exactly zero.
      </p>

      <div class="cs-diagram">
        <div class="cs-diagram-label">CinematicMonitor API — dev-only (import.meta.env.DEV)</div>
        <pre>
window.__cinematic.snapshot()   → EngineSnapshot &#123;
    fps:         &#123; avg1s: number, avg5s: number &#125;
    spikes:      &#123; count: number, worst: number &#125;    ← frames &gt; 20ms
    activeScene: string
    scrollPct:   number
    pointer:     &#123; nx: number, ny: number, active: boolean &#125;
    health:      &#123;
        scrollReady:   boolean,    ← ScrollEngine initialized
        pointerReady:  boolean,    ← PointerEngine mounted
        bootComplete:  boolean,    ← BootSystem completed
    &#125;
    subscribers: &#123;
        scroll:  number,           ← active SceneController subscribers
        pointer: number,           ← active PointerEngine subscribers
    &#125;
&#125;

window.__cinematic.history()    → SceneTransition[]  ← last 50, ring buffer
window.__cinematic.fps()        → &#123; avg1s, avg5s &#125;
window.__cinematic.health()     → EngineHealth flags
window.__cinematic.reset()      → clears accumulators
        </pre>
      </div>

      <h3 class="cs-h3" style="margin-top:40px">DebugOverlay</h3>

      <p class="cs-p">
        Toggle with <code style="font-family:var(--f-mono);font-size:0.85em;color:var(--c-cyan)">Ctrl+Shift+D</code>.
        Fixed position, top-right, 1Hz polling via setInterval (not RAF —
        zero cost when off). Displays FPS (color-coded: green &gt;50, yellow &gt;30,
        red otherwise), frame spikes, active scene, scroll %, pointer
        coordinates, and all engine health flags.
      </p>

      <div class="cs-callout">
        <p>
          <strong>Production cost:</strong>
          <code style="font-family:var(--f-mono);font-size:0.85em">import.meta.env.DEV</code>
          guards at the JSX render site cause Vite to tree-shake the entire
          DebugOverlay module at build time. CinematicMonitor class is
          present in production but all method bodies are empty no-op stubs —
          the DEV guard causes Vite to replace each branch with
          <code style="font-family:var(--f-mono);font-size:0.85em">undefined</code>.
          Zero runtime cycles in production.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── §10 · CSS Easing System ────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="cs-section">
      <div class="cs-section-eyeline">
        <span class="cs-section-num">§10</span>
        <span class="cs-section-label">CSS Easing System</span>
      </div>

      <h2 class="cs-h2">Cinematic easing token</h2>

      <p class="cs-p">
        The project uses a single easing curve across all transitions.
        It is not a preference — it is a specification. Violating it
        produces a perceptible inconsistency that reads as an accident.
      </p>

      <div class="cs-diagram">
        <div class="cs-diagram-label">Easing token definition</div>
        <pre>
--ease-cinema: cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1);

<span class="dim">/*
  This is an "expo out" approximation.
  Characteristics:
    - Very fast initial acceleration (0.16 x1)
    - Settles with an extremely long, soft tail (1.0 y1, 0.3 x2)
    - At 60fps: visually resolves in ~70% of declared duration
    - The tail creates perceived elasticity without overshoot

  Applied to:
    - All scene opacity / blur / translateY transitions
    - Hover micro-interactions (.work-cta-link, .cta-footer a)
    - Loader overlay fade
    - Case study nav hover

  Exceptions (intentional):
    - prefers-reduced-motion override blocks: bare `ease` (semantic)
    - ThreeCanvas material animation: Three.js internal
*/</span>
        </pre>
      </div>

      <h3 class="cs-h3" style="margin-top:40px">Transition duration hierarchy</h3>

      <div class="cs-diagram">
        <div class="cs-diagram-label">Duration assignments by layer</div>
        <pre>
Layer                    Property     Duration    Notes
───────────────────────  ───────────  ──────────  ──────────────────────────────
Scene transitions        opacity      1.48–1.84s  slowest layer; scene cross-dissolve
Scene transitions        blur         1.55–1.84s  slightly longer than opacity
Scene transitions        translateY   1.84–2.0s   longest; creates trailing weight
Hover micro-interactions opacity      0.2s        immediate but not instant
Hover micro-interactions color        0.2s        consistent with opacity
Boot loader              opacity      0.8s        loader fade-out feels deliberate
Nav links                color        0.3s        perceptibly responsive
        </pre>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── §11 · Accessibility Contract ──────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="cs-section">
      <div class="cs-section-eyeline">
        <span class="cs-section-num">§11</span>
        <span class="cs-section-label">Accessibility Contract</span>
      </div>

      <h2 class="cs-h2">Three degradation vectors</h2>

      <p class="cs-p">
        The cinematic experience is designed to degrade gracefully across three
        vectors. Each is handled at the appropriate layer — CSS for the
        declarative tier, JS for the runtime tier.
      </p>

      <div class="cs-decisions">

        <div class="cs-decision">
          <span class="cs-decision-status chosen">CSS</span>
          <div>
            <div class="cs-decision-what">prefers-reduced-motion — CSS layer</div>
            <div class="cs-decision-why">
              Global override in scenes.css:
              <code style="font-family:var(--f-mono);font-size:0.8em;color:var(--c-cyan)">* &#123; transition: none !important; animation: none !important; &#125;</code>
              Kills all breathing animations and CSS-driven pointer transforms.
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="cs-decision-what">Scope and trade-offs</div>
            <div class="cs-decision-why">
              The
              <code style="font-family:var(--f-mono);font-size:0.8em;color:var(--c-cyan)">!important</code>
              override is deliberately blunt — this is an accessibility
              declaration, not a style choice. The CTA availability pulse and
              philosophy breathing animations both cease entirely.
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="cs-decision">
          <span class="cs-decision-status chosen">JS</span>
          <div>
            <div class="cs-decision-what">prefers-reduced-motion — JS layer</div>
            <div class="cs-decision-why">
              PointerEngine reads
              <code style="font-family:var(--f-mono);font-size:0.8em;color:var(--c-cyan)">matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)')</code>
              and adds a change listener. If reduced motion is activated
              mid-session, pointer subscriber callbacks skip on the next
              RAF tick — no page reload required. ThreeCanvas never creates
              its WebGL renderer under reduced motion.
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="cs-decision-what">Live update model</div>
            <div class="cs-decision-why">
              The media query change listener modifies a boolean flag read
              inside the RAF loop — no engine restart, no state reset.
              Toggling OS accessibility settings takes effect within one frame.
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="cs-decision">
          <span class="cs-decision-status chosen">JS</span>
          <div>
            <div class="cs-decision-what">Coarse pointer (touch devices)</div>
            <div class="cs-decision-why">
              PointerEngine and CursorField both independently check
              <code style="font-family:var(--f-mono);font-size:0.8em;color:var(--c-cyan)">(pointer: coarse)</code>
              on mount. Either check causes the entire pointer system to be
              absent — no RAF loop, no listener, no DOM touches.
              The independent check on CursorField is deliberate: defense
              in depth rather than trusting a single gate.
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="cs-decision-what">Why absent, not degraded</div>
            <div class="cs-decision-why">
              A touch device user receives the full scene experience —
              scroll-driven transitions, atmosphere layers, all content.
              Only the pointer-responsive effects are absent.
              There is no fallback cursor shimmer or touch alternative
              because the pointer effects are enhancement only.
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── §12 · Lifecycle Contracts ──────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="cs-section">
      <div class="cs-section-eyeline">
        <span class="cs-section-num">§12</span>
        <span class="cs-section-label">Lifecycle Contracts</span>
      </div>

      <h2 class="cs-h2">Mount / unmount rules</h2>

      <p class="cs-p">
        Phase 9 discovered that lifecycle correctness is the most common
        source of subtle production bugs. These are the rules that were
        formalised after that audit.
      </p>

      <div class="cs-diagram">
        <div class="cs-diagram-label">Engine lifecycle contracts</div>
        <pre>
Engine              mount() / initialize()             unmount() / destroy()
──────────────────  ─────────────────────────────────  ──────────────────────────────────────
<span class="hl">ScrollEngine</span>        Lenis created, RAF attached,        lenis.destroy(), RAF cancelled,
                    <span class="hl">NOT</span> started (start() called later)  visibilitychange removed, _running=false

<span class="hl">PointerEngine</span>       mousemove on window (passive),       mousemove + mouseleave references
                    mouseleave on window,                removed (exact reference, not anon),
                    RAF loop started                    RAF cancelled, _lastTime reset

<span class="hl">CinematicOrchestrator</span>  run() — one call            cancel() — calls ScrollEngine.destroy()
                                                        + PointerEngine.unmount(),
                                                        _started=false (clean re-mount)
        </pre>
      </div>

      <div class="cs-callout">
        <p>
          <strong>React StrictMode double-mount:</strong>
          CinematicOrchestrator.cancel() resets
          <code style="font-family:var(--f-mono);font-size:0.85em">_started = false</code>
          so the startup pipeline runs cleanly on the second mount cycle.
          Without this reset, StrictMode's unmount+remount would leave
          the app blank — run() would see
          <code style="font-family:var(--f-mono);font-size:0.85em">_started = true</code>
          and return immediately.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

